Export async where the guest kernel says a COMMIT and a rename are durable.

A sync export makes nfsd sync every create, remove and attribute change before
answering it. On the BitLocker test drive that was 29 ms a create and 10 ms a
remove, against none with the export async: the whole cost of copying many
small files, of deleting a folder, and of Finder clearing up a cancelled copy.

It is safe only on a kernel with linux-nfsd-commit-is-durable, which syncs the
filesystem on every COMMIT and rename and keeps stable writes stable whatever
the export says, and which says so in /sys/module/nfsd/parameters. vmproxy
looks there, so a guest booted with any other kernel, such as the stock
Image-4K an f2fs volume gets, is exported sync as before. The options chosen
are printed.

Exported async, a new file's directory entry reaches the drive by writeback
unless a COMMIT or a rename comes first, so the guest's writeback runs every
second rather than after thirty.

